< Much of the web's commerce is priced by automated auction, and platforms increasingly hand the bidder the hard part of the forecast. Ad platforms and marketplaces expose a bid landscape or win-rate simulator, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice of bid to the advertiser, seller or buyer, or to the agent acting for them. Display-ad exchanges have run first-price, pay-your-bid auctions since 2019 \cite{despotakis2021firstprice}, and LLM agents are being built to bid, price and negotiate in such markets \cite{chen2024aucarena,zhu2024synthetic,ahmed2026bazaar}. Those agents increasingly run on reasoning models that spend tokens before acting, or they can call a tool for the arithmetic \cite{schick2023toolformer,gao2023pal,chen2023pot}. This Web Economics and Digital Society paper asks where the value of reasoning tokens is lost in the bid-landscape decision, and whether an agent can tell in advance when the tokens are worth spending.
