< \paragraph{Scenarios and estimator.}
< We use 628 eBay auctions of Palm Pilot PDAs, Xbox consoles and Cartier watches \cite{jank2010modeling,onlineauctionsdataset}, split 70/30 within product. A scenario gives a private value $v$, $K\in\{5,7\}$ candidate bids and, for each bid, the displayed win probability $\hat p(b)$: the empirical share of that product's construction-sample closing prices at or below $b$. This is the bid landscape. A chosen bid is scored with the same estimator on 189 held-out auctions (41--103 per product; median standard error of $p$ at a candidate bid 0.047), as expected profit $U(\hat b)=p(\hat b)(v-\hat b)$ and utility ratio $q=U(\hat b)/\max_b U(b)$. eBay prices come from proxy bidding, so closing prices are a biased estimate of first-price win rates; the models and the calculator see the same landscape and are scored the same way, so the comparison between them is unaffected. Eighteen values per product and two candidate sets per menu size give 216 scenarios. The oracle calculator returns $\arg\max_b \hat p(b)(v-b)$ and scores $q=0.981$; random choice scores 0.711. The \emph{gap recovered} at budget $c$ is $(q(c)-q(0))/(q_{\text{calc}}-q(0))$.
---
> \paragraph{Scenarios.}
> We use 628 eBay auctions of Palm Pilot PDAs, Xbox consoles and Cartier watches \cite{jank2010modeling,onlineauctionsdataset}, split 70/30 within product. A scenario gives a private value $v$, $K\in\{5,7\}$ candidate bids and, for each bid, the displayed win probability $\hat p(b)$: the share of that product's construction-sample closing prices at or below $b$. A chosen bid is scored with the same estimator on 189 held-out auctions (41--103 per product). eBay prices come from proxy bidding, so these are realistic empirical win curves rather than first-price win rates; the models and the calculator see the same landscape and are scored the same way. Eighteen values per product and two candidate sets per menu size give 216 scenarios.
> 
> \paragraph{Measures.}
> \emph{Utility ratio} $q=U(\hat b)/\max_b U(b)$ with expected profit $U(b)=p(b)(v-b)$ under held-out probabilities $p$. The oracle calculator returns $\arg\max_b \hat p(b)(v-b)$ and scores $q=0.981$; random choice scores 0.711. \emph{Gap recovered} at budget $c$ is $(q(c)-q(0))/(q_{\text{calc}}-q(0))$; the \emph{dollar gap} uses $U$ instead of $q$. \emph{Break-even} (B/e) is the smallest best expected profit per decision at which 768 tokens pay for themselves at rental prices.
