< The checkpoints are Phi-4-mini-reasoning, Qwen3-4B, Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-14B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-\allowbreak Llama-8B and Granite-3.2-8B \cite{microsoft2025phireasoning,yang2025qwen3,guo2025deepseek,ibmgranite2025}. For each budget $c\in\{0,64,128,\allowbreak 256,512,768\}$ we force exactly $c$ reasoning tokens by suppressing end-of-sequence, then read the action in a separate pass as the most likely option letter; the model's free-text answer is also recorded. We stop at 768 because a 768-token decision already takes 9--35 seconds end to end on our hardware, long when bids are placed in an auction's final seconds \cite{roth2002lastminute}, and costs under one cent at RunPod rental rates on an RTX 4090 or L40S \cite{runpod2026pricing}. Greedy decoding runs under three option orders per scenario (648 decisions per model and budget); five sampled seeds run at three budgets; an unconstrained run lets each model stop within 1{,}024 tokens. Completion at 768 tokens inferred from the unconstrained trace matches the forced trace in 96--99.8\% of cases, consistent with greedy forced traces being prefixes of unconstrained ones. A synthetic independent-private-values version with exact payoffs \cite{krishna2010auction} uses the same protocol.
