< \paragraph{The arithmetic is right; the loss is at the end.}
< Since stated values are almost always correct, the gap at 768 tokens splits into two parts (Table~\ref{tab:traces}). The first is traces that run out of tokens. Evaluating every option takes a median of 440--648 tokens with five options and 544--840 with seven (Figure~\ref{fig:main}b), so almost no trace finishes by 256 tokens. For the three Qwen models unfinished traces cause 71--95\% of the gap across parser tolerances. The second is complete traces whose final answer is not the best option they computed, the main loss for Phi-mini and Granite-8B. Ignoring follows list position, in opposite directions: Phi-mini ignores its best option in 32\% of complete traces when it is listed first and 74\% when it is listed last, Granite-8B in 79\% and 18\%. Qwen and DeepSeek ignore it in only 8--11\% of complete traces. For every family, ignoring is less likely when the best option is also the highest-probability bid (odds ratio 0.31--0.57, scenario-clustered logit) and when it clearly beats the runner-up. It is not an artifact of our letter readout: the model's own free-text answer names the calculator's bid in at most 25\% of these traces.
---
> \paragraph{Arithmetic costs one model; the rest is lost at the end.}
> Table~\ref{tab:traces} splits the 768-token gap. Arithmetic slips that change which option the trace ranks first account for 26\% of DeepSeek-8B's gap and at most 11\% for the others; on the annotated traces they change the ranking in 4\% of traces. The Qwen models lose most by running out of tokens (74--90\%): a full comparison takes a median of 440--648 tokens with five options and 544--840 with seven (Figure~\ref{fig:main}b). Phi-mini and Granite-8B lose most by passing over their own best option (75\% and 52\%). Completion does not just mark easy scenarios: within the same model and scenario, a complete trace scores 0.075 higher than an unfinished one, and the calculator is equally accurate on scenarios models usually finish and those they do not.
> 
> \paragraph{Answers pass over the best option by list position.}
> Each scenario appears in three option orders, so the list position of the best option varies within a scenario. A conditional logit with scenario fixed effects shows that moving the best option from first to last raises the odds of passing over it 12-fold for Phi-mini and 3--35-fold for the Qwen models ($p\le0.01$), lowers them to 0.06 for Granite-8B ($p<0.001$), and has no significant effect for DeepSeek-8B. When the answer passes over the best option it usually takes the runner-up (29--68\% of cases) or the highest-probability bid (19--56\%); Phi-mini's choice is the first-listed option in 38\% of these cases and Granite-8B's the last-listed in 28\%. The model's free-text answer names the calculator's bid in at most 25\% of these traces, so this is not an artifact of the letter readout.
