< \paragraph{The trace already holds most of the value.}
< Reading out the best option the trace has evaluated, with no extra tokens, recovers 80--100\% of the gap at 768 tokens across parser tolerances (Table~\ref{tab:main}) and 67--89\% at 512 tokens, where the model's own answer recovers 17--56\%. On the 150 annotated traces, the same readout built from the annotators' labels scores 0.963, against 0.959 from the parser and 0.897 for the model's answer. Finishing also counts: unconstrained traces that stopped on their own score 0.98--0.99 for five models, and within the same model and scenario a complete trace scores 0.075 higher than an unfinished one, while the calculator is equally accurate on scenarios that models usually finish and those they do not, so completion does not just mark easy scenarios.
---
> \paragraph{The trace already holds the right bid.}
> The verifier recovers 81--100\% of the gap at 768 tokens with no extra tokens, and 80--100\% across parser tolerances of 1--5\% (Table~\ref{tab:main}); built from the annotators' labels instead of the parser on the 150 annotated traces it scores 0.963, against 0.959 from the parser and 0.897 for the model's answer. It helps only once most options are evaluated: at 256 tokens it lowers $q$ by 0.002--0.046 (Figure~\ref{fig:main}c). The stricter $K-1$ variant never lowers $q$ by more than 0.001 at any budget and recovers 77--96\% at 768 tokens. Sampling is no substitute for length: two 384-token samples with majority voting score 0.06--0.18 below one 768-token sample at the same token count.
