> For $\le$14B open reasoning models making a single-bidder, pay-your-bid bid-landscape decision under budgets up to 768 forced tokens, the value of reasoning is lost mostly at the end of the trace, in different ways for different models: the Qwen models run out of tokens, while Phi-mini and Granite-8B compute the comparison and then answer against it in a way that follows list position. The information for the right bid is usually already in the trace by 768 tokens, so in this setting a lightweight verifier that reads the trace's own comparison recovers most of what a calculator would.
